= Solution
{parent=1}

Put $d=\Delta x$, $k=\Delta t$ and use the diffusion <Courant number> $r=k/d^2$. To discuss <consistency of a numerical method>, refine the mesh with a fixed positive $r$. With $\delta^2U_m=U_{m-1}-2U_m+U_{m+1}$, the printed update is $U^{n+1}-U^n=\alpha\delta^2U^{n+1}$. A <Taylor expansion> of a smooth exact solution, about the new time level, gives the residual divided by $k$:
$$
\frac{u(t+k,x)-u(t,x)-\alpha\delta^2u(t+k,x)}{k}
=\left(1-\frac{\alpha}{r}\right)u_{xx}
-\frac{k}{2}u_{xxxx}-\frac{\alpha d^4}{12k}u_{xxxx}
+O(k^2+\alpha d^6/k).
$$
Here the <heat equation> implies $u_t=u_{xx}$ and $u_{tt}=u_{xxxx}$. Since $\alpha$ depends only on $r$, the leading term can vanish for every smooth solution only if **$\alpha(r)=r$**. Thus the method is the <Backward Euler diffusion scheme>, with <local truncation error>
$$
\tau=-\left(\frac{k}{2}+\frac{d^2}{12}\right)u_{xxxx}
+O(k^2+kd^2+d^4).
$$
Its highest general accuracy is **first order in time and second order in space**, or **order two in $d$ under $k=rd^2$**. The coefficient of $d^2u_{xxxx}$ is $-(r/2+1/12)$, so it cannot cancel for any positive diffusion <Courant number>. This is a conclusion about the stencil actually printed: the new-time spatial difference fixes the sign of its temporal error. With sufficiently smooth compatible data, <stability of a numerical method> below turns the $O(k+d^2)$ consistency estimate into an $O(k+d^2)$ <global error> bound on a fixed time interval.

